# Target C — all-defect phase gap forces loaded unit sides and a linear non-tooth boundary deficit

**Date:** 2026-09-12  
**LEVEL I STATUS:** **STILL OPEN. C-II REMAINS OPEN.**  
**Status:** proof-only Type-II structural theorem; theorem authority unchanged.  
**Dependencies:** canonical Section 63; audited-working §§130 and 142; audited-working Type-II theorem `c_B=0`.

The exact pure-tooth endpoint was recently excluded by comparing its stopped-train
count with the audited-working all-defect phase gap. The same ledgers imply a much
stronger stability theorem.

Every sufficiently large Type-II survivor has a **linear population of direct unit
sides on the loaded force hull**. Consequently an asymptotically pure two-edge-tooth
boundary is impossible with a fixed linear margin.

Let

- `q` be the number of loaded force-hull vertices;
- `T_triangle` be the audited-working §130 augmented triangular-face count, equivalently
  the number of nonunit loaded-hull intervals whose distinguished outer route has
  exactly two unit edges;
- `m` be the number of nonunit loaded-hull sides;
- `r_deep:=m-T_triangle`, so `r_deep` counts nonunit loaded-hull intervals whose outer
  route has at least three edge occurrences;
- `e_BB` be the number of loaded-loaded unit contacts;
- `f` be the number of bounded nonquadrilateral core faces;
- `Q=sum_F(deg F-4)` over those faces;
- `k=d_H+s_Z`.

For sufficiently large Type-II survivors the centralized hull-side theorem gives
`c_B=0`, so every loaded-loaded unit contact is a force-hull side. Hence

\[
\boxed{e_{BB}=q-m}
\tag{1}
\]

and audited-working §130 gives

\[
\boxed{r_{deep}=m-T_{\triangle}.}
\tag{2}
\]

Therefore

\[
\boxed{
q-T_{\triangle}=e_{BB}+r_{deep}.
}
\tag{3}
\]

Thus `q-T_triangle` counts exactly the loaded-hull intervals that are not ordinary
two-edge nonunit teeth: direct unit loaded sides plus nonunit intervals whose outer
route has at least three occurrences.

## Theorem 1 — Type II has linearly many direct loaded unit sides

Every Type-II survivor sequence satisfies

\[
\boxed{
\liminf_{D\to\infty}{e_{BB}\over D}
\ge {\gamma\over2},
}
\tag{4}
\]

where the audited-working §142 constant is

\[
\gamma
:=2\sqrt{\Delta_2}-{\pi\over2},
\qquad
\Delta_2={\pi\over2\sqrt3}.
\tag{5}
\]

Numerically,

\[
\boxed{
{\gamma\over2}
=
\sqrt{\frac\pi{2\sqrt3}}-{\pi\over4}
=0.166914643466509\ldots .
}
\tag{6}
\]

Because `c_B=0`, every contact counted in (4) is an actual **unit side of the loaded
convex hull**, not an interior loaded chord.

### Proof

Canonical Section 63, specialized with `c_B=0`, gives the exact finite identity

\[
\boxed{
e_{BB}=Q+2k+u_B,}
\tag{7}
\]

with `u_B>=0`. Every bounded nonquadrilateral face has degree at least five, so

\[
f\le Q.
\tag{8}
\]

Hence

\[
\begin{aligned}
2f-k
&\le2Q-k\\
&=2(e_{BB}-2k-u_B)-k\\
&=2e_{BB}-5k-2u_B\\
&\le2e_{BB}.
\end{aligned}
\tag{9}
\]

Audited-working §142 proves the all-defect stopped-train/phase gap

\[
\boxed{
\liminf_{D\to\infty}{2f-k\over D}\ge\gamma.
}
\tag{10}
\]

Divide (9) by `2D` and apply (10). `square`

The finite inequality retained before the limit is

\[
\boxed{
e_{BB}\ge f-{k\over2}.}
\tag{11}
\]

## Theorem 2 — linear non-tooth boundary deficit

Every Type-II survivor sequence also satisfies

\[
\boxed{
\liminf_{D\to\infty}{q-T_{\triangle}\over D}
\ge {\gamma\over2}.
}
\tag{12}
\]

### Proof

Equation (3) and `r_deep>=0` give

\[
q-T_{\triangle}\ge e_{BB}.
\]

Apply Theorem 1. `square`

Equivalently, the finite chain is

\[
2(q-T_{\triangle})
=2(e_{BB}+r_{deep})
\ge2e_{BB}
\ge2f-k.
\tag{13}
\]

## Consequences

### 1. The asymptotic pure-tooth regime is impossible

There is no Type-II survivor sequence with

\[
T_{\triangle}=q-o(D).
\]

The loss from the all-two-edge-tooth boundary is at least

\[
\left(0.166914643466509\ldots-o(1)\right)D.
\]

This is strictly stronger than the exact endpoint exclusion.

### 2. The remaining full-core boundary necessarily contains a macroscopic unit-side set

Equation (4) eliminates a possible escape in which the phase-gap currency is paid
entirely by deep nonunit detours. Regardless of `r_deep`, every large Type-II survivor
has at least `(gamma/2-o(1))D` direct unit loaded-hull sides.

Those sides consume two units of the loaded contact-degree budget at every interior
vertex of a consecutive run. A closing theorem can therefore attack the interaction
between long unit-side runs, inward harmonic attachments, and the near-disk diameter
thrackle rather than treating the boundary as an arbitrary mixture of teeth and
detours.

### 3. Relation to the post-126 currency split

Audited-working §147 retains `r_deep` as a positive return-payment currency. The
present theorem adds an independent compulsory boundary currency:

\[
\boxed{
e_{BB}=\Omega(D)}
\]

with an explicit coefficient. Thus even the branch in which `r_deep=o(D)` is not a
near-pure-tooth shell; it contains a linear set of exact unit hull contacts.

## Hostile checks

- `T_triangle` is the augmented triangular-face count from §130, not the stopped-train
  count from §142.
- The conclusion that the `e_BB` contacts are hull sides uses the separately audited
  Type-II theorem `c_B=0`; outside Type II, `e_BB` may include additional loaded chords.
- The phase gap is used only in its audited-working Type-II scope.
- `f<=Q` uses only the minimum bounded nonquadrilateral degree five.
- The sign in (9) is favorable: `k,u_B>=0` are subtracted, so discarding them gives an
  upper bound on `2f-k`.
- Occurrence multiplicity and bridges are already retained in §142. Bridge excursions
  can only increase `u_B` and cannot invalidate (9).
- No contact-isolated source point is deleted or inserted into the Section-57 affine
  kernel.

**Disposition:** proof-only Type-II stability theorem. It forces a linear exact
loaded-unit-side population and eliminates the full `T_triangle=q-o(D)` triangular-
tooth regime, but it does not yet close C-II or the macroscopic inactive-container
branch.
